Note on dependencies, since the licence choice here was left open:

Every dependency is permissively licensed, so this project can be MIT, or
relicensed to anything else, without complication:

    PySide6        LGPL-3.0     Qt for Python, dynamically linked
    pyqtgraph      MIT
    numpy/scipy    BSD-3-Clause
    pandas         BSD-3-Clause
    matplotlib     PSF-like (BSD-compatible)
    seaborn        BSD-3-Clause
    openpyxl       MIT
    XlsxWriter     BSD-2-Clause
    pyserial       BSD-3-Clause
    fastapi        MIT
    uvicorn        BSD-3-Clause
    websockets     BSD-3-Clause
    zeroconf       LGPL-2.1
    platformdirs   MIT
    qasync         BSD-2-Clause
    bleak          MIT          (optional)

Two of those are copyleft, and both only in the weak sense: PySide6 (LGPL-3.0)
and zeroconf (LGPL-2.1) are used as separate, dynamically loaded libraries and
are not modified, which is exactly the arrangement the LGPL permits for a
proprietary or permissively licensed application. If you ever bundle them with
PyInstaller and ship a closed-source build, the LGPL's relinking obligation is
satisfied by offering the object files or, more simply, by noting that users can
replace the bundled libraries — worth a lawyer's glance if it matters to you.

Deliberately avoided: Qt Charts and QCustomPlot, both GPL-3.0-or-commercial,
which would have forced this project to be GPL or paid for. pyqtgraph does the
same job under MIT.
